In \TEX, nodes are an important data structure because all content is kept in (a
linked list of) nodes.
If we compare traditional \TEX\ with \LUATEX\ there are some fundamental
differences. For instance, character (glyph) nodes are
larger because we have both a prev pointer and an attribute list pointer. This
means that they are always larger than one memory word. We discuss this and
other differences in \TEX\ implementations.

What conclusions can we draw? First of all that in original \TEX\ there are
some clever memory management features. Second that in \LUATEX\ we no longer use
them. A third conclusion is that in spite of what one might think, it doesn't
impact the performance of the successor; time is likely spent elsewhere.
We leave it to the reader to wonder if the original \TEX\
approach of a single pool-with-combining-features wins over the faster but maybe
more spoiling pool-per-size approach of the other engines.
